## a b s t r a c t
Achieving a high volumetric energy density supercapacitor is of great significance for portable energy storage devices while still a major challenge. Herein, we design and fabricate self-supporting electrodes using CoZnNi oxyphosphide nanoarrays sandwiched graphene/carbon nanotube (CZNP/GC) film with highly exposed active sites. Benefitting from the modified electronic structures, high accessible surface areas, and the integrated structure, the well-designed CZNP/GC electrode exhibits an ultra-high volumetric capacitance of $2096.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at a current density of $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, a high-performance negative electrode of carbon/rGO/CNTs (C/GC) is also fabricated using the same CoZn-metal-organic frameworks precursor. The assembled asymmetric supercapacitor $\mathrm{CZNP/GC//C/GC}$ displays an ultra-high volumetric energy density of 71.8 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at 960 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ . After 6000 charge-discharge cycles, the device still maintains $85.6\%$ of the original capacitance. The density functional theory calculation is studied and the negative adsorption energy proves that the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ adsoption process onto the surface of as-prepared electrode is thermodynamically favorable, facilitating the electrochemical reaction. This work provides a new option in constructing tailorable electrodes with a well-defined hierarchical structure for supercapacitor and beyond.
