## 1. Introduction
The high volume energy density of supercapacitor has attracted significant research interest due to rising concerns on wearable and flexible devices, [1] which requires the matching electrode materials with high volume capacity and high packing density [2,3]. As a potential candidate, self-supporting integrated electrode materials could achieve a high volume capacity. Generally, nickel foam and carbon cloth are the commercial substrates widely employed in this field, but the larger volume and low specific surface area limit their volume capacity [4,5].
Graphene is an ideal substrate material for self-supporting integrated electrodes due to its superior conductivity, larger specific surface area (SSA), as well as flexibility [6,7]. Besides, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) can be intercalated between graphene sheets to avoid stacking and form a multi-directional charge transfer channel [8]. Currently, carbon-based materials are widely employed for the design of double-layer electrodes with a stable cycling life but low specific capacitance. A combination of pseudocapacitor electrode material with CNTs can improve both capacity and stability [9,10]. Liu et al. prepared a self-supporting electrode with ZnCoNiS nanosheet arrays grown on CNTs/graphene conductive substrate with a high capacitance of $1349.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ [11]. Recently, transition metal oxyphosphides had attracted considerable attention for supercapacitors due to their superior electrochemical performance [12-15]. They exhibit lower charge and discharge potentials as well as metallic-like characteristics, which can significantly enhance the surface reaction activity and reaction kinetics. Wei et al. exploited an in-situ strategy to synthesize P-doped $Z_{\mathrm{{nCo_{2}O_{4}}}}$ nanosheets on an $N$ -doped carbon skeleton with the highest capacitance of $1581.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is 3 times larger than that of P-free counterparts [16].
The electrochemical performance of electrodes largely depends on the surface area, i.e., more active sites for electrochemical reaction and more electrolyte transport paths for the diffusion/transportation of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ ions. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived metal compounds own a large SSA, good pore connectivity, and surface hydrophilicity, promoting the diffusion of ions and resulting in higher capacitance [17-19]. Yang et al. designed an electrode with a hierarchy structure, i.e., NiCoZn-layered double hydroxide (LDH) nanosheets embedded with cobalt sulfide quantum dots on carbon fibers, through a MOFs-derived and in situ selective vulcanization method. The electrode displayed an ultra-high electrochemical capacitance of $2504\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ [20]. To the best of our knowledge, MOFs-derived ternary metal oxyphosphide on the carbon-based substrate had not been reported for self-supporting electrodes in the energy storage field.
In this work, CoZnNi oxyphosphide nanoarrays sandwiched graphene/carbon nanotube (GC) film is designed reasonably with abundant exposed active sites. Firstly, the nanoarray structure facilitates the infiltration and permeation of electrolytes. Secondly, the internal 3D network structure of GC provides mechanical flexibility avoiding the destruction of the film during the electrochemical process. Thirdly, P-doping could effectively boost the oxidation/reduction activity and further enhance the capacitance. The density functional theory (DFT) calculations indicate that the CoZnNi oxyphosphide possesses a higher favored $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ adsorption, which is thermodynamically advantageous in electrochemical reaction. The well-designed self-supporting electrode delivers an ultrahigh volumetric capacitance of $2096.4\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, a high-performance negative electrode porous carbon/rGO/CNTs $({\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{G C}})$ is also fabricated using the same CoZnMOF precursor for the assembly of an asymmetric supercapacitor, which displays a super-high volumetric energy density of 71.8 W $\mathrm{~h~L~}^{-1}$ at $960\mathrm{~W~L-}^{1}$ . After 6000 cycles, the stability of the device remains $85.6\%$ of the initial capacitance. The density functional theory (DFT) calculations indicate that the $\mathsf{C o Z n N i}$ oxyphosphide possesses a higher thermodynamically favored $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ adsorption, which boosts electrochemical reaction. Our approach provides a facile strategy in constructing tailorable electrodes with a welldefined hierarchical structure based on ternary metal oxyphosphide for supercapacity in the energy storage field.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
The analytical-grade chemicals used here were without further purification. $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{NiSO}_{4}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathsf{N a H}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{PO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , ${\ K}_{2}S_{2}{\ O}_{8}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4},$ ethanol, and $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ $(28\%)$ are purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) was obtained from Aladdin. Carbon nanotubes (CNTs, $1.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) are purchased from XFNANO Company Limited.
## 2.2. Synthesis
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of CoZn-MOF/graphene oxide (GO)/carbon nanotubes (CNTs)
GO dispersion $(0.2\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}.$ is synthesized according to the method previously reported [21]. The GO/CNTs substrate is obtained by vacuum filtering the mixed dispersion of CNTs $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and GO ( $25~\mathrm{mL}$ . Next, $0.59\textrm{g C o}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $0.30\mathrm{~g~Zn~}$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , and $1.2\mathrm{~g~}2$ -MIM were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, respectively. Mixing above two nitrate solutions for a while and adding 2-MIM solution into the mixed liquor to prepare the CoZn-MOF. After that, the GO/CNTs substrate was soaked into the as-prepared CoZn-MOF dispersion for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ to obtain CoZnMOF/GO/CNTs.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of CoZnNiOP@GO/CNTs
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of the porous carbon/rGO/CNTs negative electrode
The CoZn-MOF/GO/CNTs precursor was annealed at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain CoZnO/rGO/CNTs. Then, the obtained film was added into sulfuric acid $(50\%)$ and reacted in an oil bath at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the treated samples were purified using deionized water and ethanol several times to obtain the carbon/rGO/CNTs $[{\cal C}]$ GC) negative electrode. The packing density of ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{C}}$ is calculated to be $1.20\ \mathrm{g\cm}^{-3}$ .
## 2.3. Material characterizations
The composition and phase of materials are characterized by Xray diffraction (XRD, D/MAX-2500) with a radiation source $\operatorname{Cu}\ K\alpha,$ $\lambda=1.54056\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). The cross-section and top-view morphology of the films are observed by the Hitachi S-4800 field emission scanning electronic microscope (FESEM). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) is used to characterize the specific features of the samples using F20 S-TWIN (Tecnai G2, American, FEI Co.) under an acceleration voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}.$ The chemical valence state analysis of the product elements was investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) with an $\mathsf{X}$ -ray source of 150 WAI (ESCALAB 250X America).
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurement
All the electrochemical tests of the products were tested by a CHI 760E electrochemical station (Shanghai Chenhua). In a threeelectrode system, the as-prepared film (CZNP/GC, ZCNO/GC, and $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{G C})$ is sandwiched between two pieces of nickel foams $(1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and used as a working electrode, the platinum plate is used as a counter electrode, and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode is used as the reference electrode. The electrolyte is KOH aqueous solution (6 M).
In a two-electrode system, the CZNP/GC film $1\ \mathrm{cm}\times1\ \mathrm{cm};$ is directly used as the positive electrode, and the $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{G C}$ film $(1\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 1\ \mathrm{cm})$ is used as the negative electrode. The electrolyte is a $6\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{KOH}$ aqueous solution. The membrane is a cellulosic separator. The electrochemical performance was estimated by the cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD), and electrochemical impedance spectrometry (EIS).
## 3. Results and discussion
The stepwise synthesis of CoZnNiOP/rGO/CNTs film is described in Fig. 1. The GO/CNTs substrate is assembled through filtrating the mixture of carbon nanotubes and GO dispersion. Then, Co-Zn MOF arrays were grown on both sides of the carbon substrate by a solution method. A CoZnNiOP/rGO/CNTs composite electrode is formed after ion exchange, annealing, and further phosphating.
The structure and morphology of the as-synthesized films are firstly characterized by FESEM. The cross-section SEM image in Fig. 2a indicated that the sandwich-structural GC substrate is composed of graphene nanosheets and carbon nanotubes, where both of them are evenly intertwined together. This structure can provide a sufficient space for the growth of MOFs and promote the rapid transfer of electrolytes between the electrode material. CoZnMOF nanosheets grow on both sides of the substrate, resulting in a $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ thick composite film. The top-view SEM image in Fig. 2b showed a smooth triangular-shaped CoZn-MOF nanosheets array vertically grown on the GC substrate’s surface. Fig. 2c-e is the SEM images including cross-section and top-view of the $\mathsf{C Z N P}/$ GC, where the sandwich-structural film was maintained except for a slightly thinner thickness after a series of reactions. Fig. 2de displays that the product’s surface is composed of loose and porous connected fluffy triangle structures because some of Zn and Co ions had been replaced by Ni ions during the ion exchange process and some of O is replaced by P during the phosphating process. This changed surface provides more electrochemically active sites and facilitates the transport of electrolytes. We further measured the surface area of the composites by nitrogen adsorptiondesorption isotherm (Fig. S2) and it is $41.7\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which can provide more active sites for electrochemical reaction. The inset in Fig. 2d is a digital picture of the entire membrane, showing its certain flexibility and the potential for flexible electrode design. Fig. 2f is the top-view SEM image of ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{C}}$ porous structure with different magnification. It is observed that after a series of chemical treatment, the surface of the film has changed from the original triangular sheet structure to the porous network structure. This structure feature can provide more active sites using as the negative electrode.
Fig. 3a is the TEM image of a detached triangular structural CZNP nanosheet with a size of about $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The size is consistent with the above SEM images. The HRTEM image of CZNP in Fig. 3b image indicated that the nanosheet consists of round nanoparticles and space conducive to electrolyte diffusion between active substances. HRTEM image in Fig. 3c clearly shows lattice fringes with distances of $0.24\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.16\mathrm{nm}$ , which matches well with the (3 1 1) and (2 1 1) crystal structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and NiCoP material, respectively. Fig. 3e-i are the elemental mapping images of the CZNP, indicating the homogeneous composition element of Zn, Ni, Co, O, and P in nanosheets.
The phase of the synthesized material was analysed by XRD, as shown in Fig. S1. It is found that the prepared material is a mixture phase, corresponding to three substances of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (PDF#78–1970), NiCoP (PDF#71–2336), and ZnO (PDF#74–0534). The elemental composition and chemical valence states of CZNP/GC are further analysed using XPS. XPS survey spectrum with characteristic peaks of Zn 2p, Ni 2p, Co 2p, O 1 s, C 1 s, and $\mathtt{P2p}$ in Fig. 4a is consistent with EDS results. High-resolution spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ in Fig. 4b is divided into four peaks, where the binding energies at $856.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ of $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $873.8{\ \mathrm{eV}}$ of $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ correspond to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , and the peaks located at $858.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ of $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $875.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ of $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ are attributed to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}\left[22,23\right]$ . The Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ peaks at the binding energies of 781.4 and $798.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ belong to $C o^{2+}$ , while the binding energy $775.5\mathrm{eV}$ of Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $797.0\mathrm{eV}$ of Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ are corresponding to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ in Fig. 4c [24]. $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ spectrum in Fig. 4d presents two typical strong peaks at 1021.9 eV and 1045.1 eV corresponding to Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively [25]. The P 2p spectrum in Fig. 3e is fitted with three peaks at 133.4 eV, 134.1 eV, and $134.8\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the presence of $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2},2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ , and $\mathsf{P}{\-}-0$ , respectively [26]. The O 1 s spectrum in Fig. 4f is fitted into three peaks, indexing to the $c{-}0$ , adsorbed $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ , and $\mathsf{P}{-}0$ , respectively [27].
The electrochemical performance of the electrode is firstly carried out in a three-electrode system. Fig. 5a presents the CV curves of CZNP/GC and CZNO/GC electrodes under a low scan rate of $2\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . The obvious cathodic and anodic peaks indicate the existence of redox reaction in the inner part of the film electrode. The CZNP/GC electrode presents a larger area from the CV curves due to the enhancement of electron and ion transport rate by $\mathbf{P}$ -doping, which enhance the additional Faradaic reaction. Fig. 5b is the GCD curves of ${\mathsf{C Z N P/G C}}$ and CZNO/GC electrodes at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , where the appearance of the voltage platform also indicates the pseudocapacitance characteristics. Consistent with the CV results, $\mathsf{C Z N P}/$ GC electrode shows a longer discharge time, indicating its higher electrochemical capacitance. Complete CV and GCD curves of the above two electrodes are shown in Fig. 5c, 5d, and Fig. S4, where CZNP/GC electrode always shows a better capacity. Fig. 5e is the gravimetric, volumetric, and area capacitance of CZNP/GC film calculated from Fig. 5d. At the current densities of $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $1.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , $2.5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , $4\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , and $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , the gravimetric (volumetric and area) capacitance of the CZNP/GC electrode are $1416.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(2096.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $1.05\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ), $1155.9\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(1710.7\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $0.85\ \mathrm{~F~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ , $940.4\mathrm{~F~}\ g^{-1}$ $(1391.8~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $0.69\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ), $867.6~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ( $1284.1~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $0.64~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), $825.2~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ $(1221.3\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $0.61\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ , and $772.6\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $1143.4\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $0.57~\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , respectively. The volumetric capacitance of CZNP/GC electrode is much higher than those of the previous reported carbon-based electrodes shown in Table 1. To further analyse the underlying reasons for the excellent performance, impedance tests were performed in Fig. 5f. The diameter of the semicircular curve always represents the charge transfer resistance $(R_{c t})$ . The EIS fitted data is shown in Table S1. CZNP/GC electrode has a smaller $R_{c t}$ $\mathbf{\Omega}^{\prime}0.6247\ \Omega$ than that of CZNO/GC electrode $(0.9564\ \Omega)$ , indicating a higher electric conductivity. Besides, an almost vertical line can be observed in the low-frequency region, indicating that our sandwiched structure electrode is conducive to the transport of electrolytes.
To further explore and analyze the electrochemical performance of CZNP/GC electrode, CV tests at various low scanning rates from 2 to $5{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ were performed in Fig. 6a. The relationship between different peaks current $(I_{\mathrm{p}},\mathsf{A})$ and scan rate $(\nu,\mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}),$ 1 can be gained by the formula as follow: [21]
$$
i\ =\ a\nu^{b}
$$
In this formula, b is the adjustable parameters. There is a diffusion-controlled process, and the electrode material expressed as battery type; if the value of b is within the scope of $0.5\sim1.0$ , it is diffusion-controlled process and pseudocapacitive effect co-exist at the same time; ${\sf b}=1.0$ means that the electrode has a pseudocapacitance property. Fig. 6b shows the anodic and cathodic peaks are 0.7513 and 0.7785, respectively, indicating a behavior combination of battery and pseudocapacitance characteristics.
The capacitance contribution in the electrochemical reaction process is calculated using the following formula: [34]
$$
I/\nu^{0.5}=k_{1}\nu^{0.5}+k_{2}
$$
where $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ represent adjustable constants, $I$ is the current at the stable potential. And $\bf k_{1}v$ is related to the pseudocapacitive capacitance contribution under every specific voltage. For the CV curves in Fig. 6c-e, the pseudocapacitive contribution of the electrode material is $38.5\%$ , $42.5\%$ and $50.1\%$ at $2\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}s^{-1}$ , $3\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , and $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}s^{-1}$ , respectively. Besides, Fig. 6f shows the proportion of capacitance contribution becomes larger and larger following the increasing scan rate, which means that the larger scanning rate is more beneficial to the diffusion of electrolyte and shortening transmission path.
It is well known the electrochemical reaction of transition metal oxides (phosphides) in the alkalinity system is related to the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ adsorption [26]. The density functional theory (DFT) calculations are applied to further reveal the insight electrochemical reaction mechanism of CZNO and CZNP electrode. DFT calculation details are described in supporting information. Fig. 7a and b show the crystallographic models of CZNO, CZNO-OH, CZNP, and CZNP-OH. The computation result is shown in Fig. 7c. The simulated $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ adsorption energies of CZNO and CZNP are $0.255~\mathrm{~eV}$ and 0.057 eV, respectively, indicating that the CZNP possesses a higher favored $\mathrm{\DeltaOH^{-}}$ adsorption. Therefore, according to the simulation results of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ adsorption energy, CZNP is more thermodynamically advantageous in electrochemical reaction compared with that of CZNO.
As shown in Fig. 8a, the as-prepared electrodes are assembled into a supercapacitor to evaluate the practical potential. The possible voltage window is determined by the CV curves of the positive and negative materials. From Fig. 8b, the potential range of $\mathsf{C Z N P}/$ GC is $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ while the voltage window of ${\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{C}}$ is $-1.0\substack{-0\vee}$ In consequence, the voltage window of the asymmetric supercapacitor may exceed $1.5~\mathrm{V}.$ We collected the CV curves of the device under different voltage windows in Fig. 8c. The voltage range of the device can be extended to $1.7~\mathrm{V}$ but polarization will occur when the voltage exceeds this range. Fig. S4 shows the complete CV and GCD curves of the $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{G C}$ negative electrode. The curves have good symmetry, and no obvious redox peak is observed, indicating that C/GC electrode has good electrochemical double-layer capacitance characteristics, and is an ideal negative electrode with the maximum capacity of $202.1\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ . Fig. 8d is the CV curves of the device at the scan rates of $2{-}50\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . All the CV curves exhibit a relatively stable shape at different scan rates, indicating good charge and discharge capabilities and ideal reversibility. Fig. 8e is the GCD curves of the device tested at different current densities $(1-10\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1};$ . The good symmetry of the curves indicates that the device has high-coulombic efficiency. The specific capacitances (Fig. 8f) corresponding to GCD curves are $168.6\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (202.3 $\mathsf{F}\mathsf{c m}^{-3}.$ ), $149.4\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $179.3\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ , $131.4\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $(157.7\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}.$ ), $109.1\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ $130.9\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3},$ ), $101.8\mathsf{F}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ $(122.1~\mathrm{F~cm}^{-3}.$ ), and 94.5 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $113.4\ \mathrm{F\cm}^{-3}.$ at the current densities of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , 2 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , $3\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The Coulomb efficiency of $\mathrm{CZNP/GC//C/GC}$ asymmetric supercapacitor is $60.7\%$ and $95.0\%$ at $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
The gravimetric energy density and volumetric energy density of this asymmetric supercapacitor are also calculated and compared with the previous literature. Our device exhibits a maximum gravimetric energy density of $59.9\mathsf{W h}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ at $800\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ (Fig. 9a) and a volumetric energy density of 71.8 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at 960 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ (Fig. 9b). The performance is comparable with or better than those of other carbon-based supercapacitors in the reported literature, like CCO/CO/MW//AC (60.5 W h $\log^{-1}$ at 750 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [35], $\mathsf{V O C N T@C O_{0.85}S e/N F//A C}$ (21.47 W h $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at 325 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [36], CC/CoNi-MOF//g-CNT (55.5 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at 175.5 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [37], NiONSs@CNTs@CuO $\mathsf{V W A s/C u//A C@C}$ (26.32 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at 219.03 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [38], CCS//CNC (38.4 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $750~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}})$ [39], $N{-}\mathrm{GQD}@\mathrm{cZIF-}8/\mathrm{GH}//N{-}\mathrm{GQD}@\mathrm{cZIF-}8/\mathrm{GH}$ (18.1 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at 4907.3 W ${\sf L}^{-1}$ ) [40], CoZnNiS@CNTs/rGO//CG (65.2 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ at $1308\mathrm{~W~L^{-1}}.$ ) [11], rGO/CNT//AC (12.3 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ at 506.7 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [41], PUCNT/RGO//PUCNT/RGO (8.63 W h ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ zat $2031.2\mathrm{~W~L^{-1}}.$ 1 [42], (CNTs) $\mathrm{|{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}//(C N T s)/{N i C o_{2}O_{4}}}$ (1.17 W h ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ at 2430 W ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) [43]. To explore the practical application potential of this device, 1 white and 12 blue LEDs are lighted by these two supercapacitors in series. Fig. 9c reveals that the intensity of the lights retains at the first $30~s$ and then dim gradually after the $90~s.$ Fig. 9d presents that the $\mathsf{C Z N P/G C//C/G C}$ supercapacitor has a good cyclic stability of $85.6\%$ initial capacity retention after 6000 cycles. All the above result indicates that this supercapacitor has the potential to be employed for practical application in energy storage systems.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, we designed a self-supporting hierarchy structural electrode based on ternary transition metal oxyphosphide (CoZnNiOP) nanoarrays grown on both sides of graphene/carbon nanotubes (GC) substrate. The as-prepared composite electrode exhibits an ultra-high specific volumetric capacitance of 2096.4 $\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ owing to the highly exposed active sites and excellent electrical conductivity. Moreover, an asymmetric supercapacitor is assembled using a high-performance porous carbon/GC-based negative electrode, which is fabricated using the same CoZn-based precursor. The assembled CoZnNiOP/GC//carbon/GC supercapacitor displays an ultra-high volumetric energy density and stable cycle performance, which successfully light a device with 1 white and 12 blue LEDs. The density functional theory analysis is performed to investigate the underlying mechanism of the superior electrochemical capacity performance, where the $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ adsorption process spontaneously occurs in the CoZnNiOP system. The excellent electrochemical performance of the as-assembled supercapacitor indicates this design approach has huge advantages and holds the potential in future energy storage devices.
